{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The code is meant to be thrown away, so the only thing that matters is whether the idea works quickly and cheaply. Reading or cleaning up AI output adds friction for zero lasting benefit, so accept it as-is and judge it purely by behavior."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "You need a demo that reliably tells the story to stakeholders or investors, and a broken demo is a real cost. Have AI write the code, then review selectively and verify the demo path with tests so it holds up when someone else drives it."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real early users depend on it, so correctness and behavior matter, but speed still dominates because scope and requirements are changing fast. Review selectively and lean on tests for behavior; keep line-by-line rigor for the parts that touch data, money, or auth."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintained codebase mean you own this code for years, so bugs, security holes, and unreadable AI output turn into ongoing costs. AI can write it, but a person should review every change line by line before it ships."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated, safety-critical, or otherwise high-consequence systems demand accountability and auditability that black-box code cannot provide. Let AI suggest, but a person must write or rewrite every line so that a named human understands and owns what runs."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding should decrease as the cost of being wrong increases: full vibe coding (4) is fine for throwaway prototypes, selective-review-with-tests (3) suits demos and early MVPs where speed still matters, line-by-line review (2) is the floor once paying customers and long-term maintenance are involved, and critical systems should cap at AI-suggests-human-writes (1) for accountability. The organizing principle is that unread code is a liability you're choosing to accept; accept it heavily where the code is disposable and the blast radius is small, and stop accepting it where a real user, a regulated process, or a safety outcome can be harmed."
}
